      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the number of distinct numbers in the list \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ldots, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\), we need to analyze the floor function and the range of the values produced.\n\nFirst, let's write the general term of the sequence:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\n\\]\nwhere \\(n\\) ranges from 1 to 1000. We need to determine how many distinct values this sequence takes.\n\nConsider the function \\(f(n) = \\frac{n^2}{1000}\\). As \\(n\\) increases from 1 to 1000, \\(f(n)\\) will take on slightly different integer values. The floor function takes the greatest integer less than or equal to a given number. Therefore, we need to find the range of \\(f(n)\\) for \\(n\\) from 1 to 1000.\n\nThe smallest value of \\(f(n)\\) occurs when \\(n = 1\\):\n\\[\nf(1) = \\frac{1^2}{1000} = \\frac{1}{1000}\n\\]\nand the largest value of \\(f(n)\\) occurs when \\(n = 1000\\):\n\\[\nf(1000) = \\frac{1000^2}{1000} = 1000\n\\]\nThis means that \\(f(n)\\) ranges from \\(\\frac{1}{1000}\\) to 1000 with \\(f(n)\\) taking each integer value in this range.\n\nTo find the number of distinct integer values that \\(f(n)\\) can take, we need to find the maximum integer value less than or equal to 1000. The greatest integer less than or equal to 1000 is 1000 itself. So, \\(f(n)\\) takes on all the integers from 1 to 1000.\n\nThus, the number of distinct numbers in the list is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1000}\n\\]",
